on kernels (residual-add+RMSNorm producing the running residual and the next normed input; per-head q/k RMSNorm + NeoX rotary + head-split writing q in [1,32,256,128] and k/v directly into the final [1,8,256,128] cache layout, eliminating the parent's transpose/cat copies; SiLU*gate on the packed gate_up GEMM output) that reproduce the parent's bf16 materialization points (fp32 accumulation, bf16 rounding after each parent-visible elementwise op, torch-style RMSNorm rounding chain, rot = cat(-x2,x1) with the same intermediate roundings); (c) keeps the parent's math library calls unchanged - cuBLAS mm for o/down/lm_head, flash SDPA with is_causal=True, scale=head_dim**-0.5, enable_gqa=True, dropout 0 (the parent sdpa interface dispatches to pytorch_flash::flash_fwd_splitkv_kernel for this shape), embedding gather, final RMSNorm on the last row (row-wise equivalent to the parent's norm-then-slice) and the same [1,1,vocab] bf16 lm_head GEMM; (d) builds the cos/sin tables once from the module's own inv_freq buffer with the parent's fp32 outer-product math, so they are bit-identical to the per-invocation parent tables. The whole region is lazily captured into a CUDA graph inside the first real invocation (after default-stream Triton compile, 3 side-stream warmups and an explicit synchronize) and replayed on every subsequent invocation after copying the current input ids into the static buffer, so the workload executes on the live inputs each time; an eager composite and the parent forward remain as graded fallbacks (graph -> eager -> parent), with distinct route labels (fused_prefill_graph_replay / fused_prefill_eager_composite / parent_signature_fallback / parent_runtime_fallback / setup_failure_parent_fallback).\n\nExpected effect (hypothesis to be validated by the framework's paired measurement, not a claim): ttft_ms 27.681 -> roughly 17-21 ms, from removing the ~6 ms dispatch gap via replay, ~1.2 ms GPU from the fused QKV GEMM (k/v GEMMs run at ~48 TFLOPS effective at N=1024), and ~3-4 ms GPU from replacing the ~5 ms pointwise chain with ~1 ms of fused kernels; GEMM math is preserved so decode_tokens_per_second should be unchanged within noise (the 31 decode steps stay on the untouched parent path).\n\nWhy consumers of the eliminated calls remain valid: the only consumers of the region's inner calls are the region itself and the adapter's prefill phase, which consumes output.logits ([1,1,vocab] bf16, same GEMM as parent) and output.past_key_values. My replacement returns a standard transformers DynamicCache (config-typed, 36 DynamicLayers, keys/values [1,8,256,128] contiguous, is_initialized) exactly matching the parent prefill cache contract; the untouched parent decode loop reads get_seq_length()=256 and extends it with torch.cat per step, unchanged. All 3642 eliminated calls are prefill-phase calls inside the replaced subtree; no decode-phase or mixed-phase call is eliminated (the decode-phase calls of the same physical modules are separate sites and remain on the parent). State lifecycle and synchronization: setup happens at installation (weight repack, rope tables) and on the first invocation (Triton compile, warmup, capture; a one-time cost absorbed by warmup=2); steady-state invocations add no synchronization - replay is enqueued on the current stream and the adapter's own explicit torch.cuda.synchronize still defines the ttft boundary; each invocation receives a fresh DynamicCache object over graph-written static buffers (decode only reads them via cat), and the returned logits are cloned out of the static buffer. CPU verification: on a CPU clone of the exact model class the torch-fallback composite reproduces the parent prefill logits and cache bit-identically and continues decode through the returned cache bit-identically; the Triton path matches within interpreter bf16 emulation noise.",
